{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017T7R9ZWprMGJtVNrDjJmfD","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (43.7KB). Full output saved to: /ard-home/.claude/projects/-Users-al--ard-work-albertcbrown-ard-01M84R5GDB44NC8C0BT75DDE0V/b768ecb7-c87b-4dd5-9df7-ee39a31a7137/tool-results/bw9aocwy1.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\n50:Every label starts with `ard:`, and every stage uses one grammar: `ard:<stage>:<role>` requests work, `:running` marks a claim, `:done` marks completion. A loop picks up an issue that has the request label and none of `:running`, `:done`, `ard:executor:failed`, `ard:needs-human` or `ard:hold`.\n63:        ard:hold, ard:needs-human and ard:pause\n81:| `ard:needs-human` | Controller, from the agent's result | Agent reported it is blocked; automation stops |\n87:**One query per role per poll.** For example: `is:open label:\"ard:triage:architect\" -label:\"ard:triage:architect:running\" -label:\"ard:triage:architect:done\" -label:ard:executor:failed -label:ard:needs-human -label:ard:hold`. Six triage roles every 15 minutes, plus four reviewers, a worker and the merger every 5, is about 100 searches an hour, well under GitHub's limits.\n127:A worker's inputs are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md`, `comments.md` and, on a fix step, `test-output.log` (written by the loop, never committed) and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`. Nobody may change `issue.md` (only the scanner writes it, through the committer, not close-out), another role's file, any other file, or delete `plan.md`. A job that owns a `plan.md` section may add, replace in place, move or remove that section; a change to the text before the first `##` heading is allowed only when the job creates the file. Headings match exactly (case-sensitive), and `##` lines inside a fenced code block are text, not headings. A change outside the allowlist fails the job as `needs-human`: the comment names up to three offending paths, and only the redacted log is committed, never the offending files. A job with no valid role file still fails as `failed`, because the allowlist is checked after the role file parses. `logs/` is the controller's: files an agent writes there are ignored, not committed. `costs/` is the controller's too, but stricter: no role may write it, so an agent write there breaks the allowlist like any other path and fails the job as\n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"72a1a7d6-7fdc-45ac-9b6e-7e6bb020d70e","uuid":"3eb11933-ae01-4210-8d38-b37a639a37e8","timestamp":"2026-11-23T17:48:46.498Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"50:Every label starts with `ard:`, and every stage uses one grammar: `ard:<stage>:<role>` requests work, `:running` marks a claim, `:done` marks completion. A loop picks up an issue that has the request label and none of `:running`, `:done`, `ard:executor:failed`, `ard:needs-human` or `ard:hold`.\n63:        ard:hold, ard:needs-human and ard:pause\n81:| `ard:needs-human` | Controller, from the agent's result | Agent reported it is blocked; automation stops |\n87:**One query per role per poll.** For example: `is:open label:\"ard:triage:architect\" -label:\"ard:triage:architect:running\" -label:\"ard:triage:architect:done\" -label:ard:executor:failed -label:ard:needs-human -label:ard:hold`. Six triage roles every 15 minutes, plus four reviewers, a worker and the merger every 5, is about 100 searches an hour, well under GitHub's limits.\n127:A worker's inputs are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md`, `comments.md` and, on a fix step, `test-output.log` (written by the loop, never committed) and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`. Nobody may change `issue.md` (only the scanner writes it, through the committer, not close-out), another role's file, any other file, or delete `plan.md`. A job that owns a `plan.md` section may add, replace in place, move or remove that section; a change to the text before the first `##` heading is allowed only when the job creates the file. Headings match exactly (case-sensitive), and `##` lines inside a fenced code block are text, not headings. A change outside the allowlist fails the job as `needs-human`: the comment names up to three offending paths, and only the redacted log is committed, never the offending files. A job with no valid role file still fails as `failed`, because the allowlist is checked after the role file parses. `logs/` is the controller's: files an agent writes there are ignored, not committed. `costs/` is the controller's too, but stricter: no role may write it, so an agent write there breaks the allowlist like any other path and fails the job as `needs-human`; the controller stages its own `costs/<job-id>.json` after that check, and it is never on `closeout.WritableFor`. The allowlist is code (`closeout.WritableFor`), not config; `plan_section` only names the heading a role owns. Workers and reviewers get their inputs outside `.ard/<issue#>/` (for example `.ard-input/<issue#>/`), because anything they write under it counts as their change. `issue.md` is the issue body without ard's cost block (see Guardrails), so the table never reaches an agent; the reviewer's untrusted `.ard/<issue#>/` input includes `costs/*.json`, which is controller-written and holds no free text.\n138:status: done          # done | blocked | needs-human (both of the last two add ard:needs-human)\n156:**All agent output is committed too.** Each run's full output lands in `.ard/<issue#>/logs/<stage>-<role>-<ts>.log`. Close-out redacts known secret values from the whole stream and, in the same pass, replaces the controller host's own paths with fixed placeholders: the job worktree becomes `/work`, the job home `/ard-home`, the executor root `~/.ard`, the operator's home `~` and the controller's temp dir `$TMPDIR` (skipped when it is a shared `/tmp`). Each path is also replaced in its symlink-resolved form, longest first, and only where it ends at a path boundary; bare usernames are not touched. Close-out then secret-scans the whole redacted log (the scan sees the replaced text), then caps it at 1 MiB: the first 32 KiB plus the last ~992 KiB, cut on line boundaries and joined by one truncation marker line. The full redacted log stays host-local for 7 days. Git LFS is not used (decided, #4); revisit only if `.git` growth demands it. Once the redacted copy is committed, the raw log is deleted. Triage output goes to `main` through the committer; worker and reviewer output goes on the branch and ships in the PR. **Scanner run logs stay host-local in phase 0** (`~/.ard/logs/`, 7 days) and are not committed: one run can touch many issues, and close-out redaction and the secret scan (#20, #21) land after Gate 1. What a scanner run commits is `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` for each issue it files or joins, nothing else. Committing scanner logs, redacted, to each touched issue is a follow-up. Never committed: scanner state databases, and any log the secret scan flags. **Optional gitleaks pass (#89).** With `redact.gitleaks.enabled: true` (default false; `redact.gitleaks.path` names the pinned binary, default `gitleaks` on PATH, and is read at each close-out) the scan runs a second pass after the builtin one: `gitleaks detect --no-git --report-format json` over the redacted bytes, in a private temp dir with a scrubbed environment and a 2-minute timeout. Findings keep only the rule, line and column, never the matched text. The pass fails closed: a missing binary, an error exit, a timeout or an unreadable report means nothing is committed. A hit is treated like an internal hit: the log stays host-local for 7 days and the job gets `ard:needs-human`.\n175:**The output schema is strict.** Each line is one JSON object with exactly these keys; blank lines are skipped. `fingerprint` (required, 1 to 512 bytes of valid UTF-8, no control characters), `title` (required, one line, at most 200 characters), `severity` (required: `critical`, `high`, `medium`, `low` or `info`), `group` (optional; empty means one issue per finding), `files` (optional repo-relative paths, clean, no `..`) and `body` (optional markdown, at most 64 KiB). An unknown key, a bad value, a duplicate fingerprint, a line over 1 MiB, a stream over 16 MiB or more than 10,000 findings is an invalid line, and **any invalid line fails the whole run**: nothing is filed, closed or changed, so a half-parsed run can never trigger autoclose. Titles, bodies and file names come from repository content, so the controller treats them as untrusted data: before they reach an issue body it escapes HTML comment delimiters, so scanner output cannot forge a marker.\n180:2. **Backpressure, in code, before the scanner starts.** The controller counts open issues with `ard:scanner:<name>`; at or above `max_open` it skips the run and logs it, autoclose scanners included: a scanner at its cap does not run with a zero filing budget, so it cannot autoclose its own fixed issues until a human or the stale closer frees a slot. A global cap across all scanners (`global_max_open`) stops a bad day from burying the queue. A skipped run is not queued: it counts as a run, and the next scheduled run checks again.\n182:4. **Diff.** A new, unsuppressed fingerprint is queued for filing. A suppressed fingerprint stays quiet unless its severity rises above the suppressed one. A fingerprint that returns after being fixed files a new issue that links the old one as a regression. An open issue whose fingerprints are all gone gets \"No longer detected at `<sha>`\" and is closed, but only for scanners marked `autoclose` (deterministic ones); other scanners never edit or close an issue for absence. A returning finding unticks its line.\n183:5. **Group.** Findings that share a `group` key join one issue, up to `group_size` (N), as a checklist with one line per finding (ticked lines count towards N). An empty `group` means one issue per finding. New findings join an open group issue of the same group that has room and is not yet claimed, meaning it carries no `ard:` label besides `ard:scanner:<name>` and `ard:pending`; otherwise they start a new one. A regression never joins: it always opens a new issue, which may also hold new findings of the same group. The number of new issues per run is `min(max_open - open, global_max_open - global open)`; joins are free. Findings over that budget are filed most severe first, and the rest stay unrecorded so the next run offers them again.\n198:**The stale closer is a built-in scanner.** It has no role directory and needs no job slot, executor or worktree; its settings live in the top-level `stale:` block of `config.yaml`. It is a plain controller loop that runs every 24 hours and is skipped while paused. It closes scanner issues still at `ard:pending` with no activity for `stale.inactive_for` (default 30 days), as not planned, with a comment. Activity means a timeline event by anyone other than the ard App. It never closes an issue that has a `:running` label, `ard:hold`, `ard:needs-human`, a linked open PR, or a human author (any author other than the App), and it closes nothing when the client is not authenticated as an App. It only comments and closes: suppression happens on the owning scanner's next reconcile, which suppresses every not-planned close for `stale.suppress_for`. A reopened issue gets a fresh comment when it goes stale again.\n214:**The error-codes scanner** (`scanners/error-codes`, a `sh` script plus one `jq` program, pattern match over source, registry and docs files) reads a human-owned `codes.json` in its role directory; nothing ships one, because error-code conventions are specific to each repository. Keys: `pattern` (required, a regex of at most 512 characters, wrapped so `E1001` does not match inside `XE10011`; it must not match the empty string), `registry` (required, a list of path patterns), `entry` (optional: a registry line must also match it to define a code), `docs` (optional; unset turns the undocumented check off), `sources` (optional, default every text file) and `exclude`. A path pattern ending in `/` is a directory prefix from the root, one without `/` matches the base name, and any other is a glob on the whole path (`*` and `?` stop at `/`, `**` does not). Unknown keys, wrong types and lists over 100 entries are errors. Codes found in registry files (on `entry` lines) are R, codes anywhere in docs files are D, codes in every other text file are S. Findings: `error-codes:duplicated:<code>` (high, group `duplicate codes`: a code on two or more distinct registry lines), `error-codes:unregistered:<code>` (medium, group `unregistered codes`: in S, not in R) and `error-codes:undocumented:<code>` (low, group `undocumented codes`: in R, not in D, only when `docs` is set). Fingerprints use the code alone, so moving a use never churns an issue. `.git`, `.ard`, `vendor`, `node_modules`, `testdata`, symlinks, binary files, files of about 1 MiB or more and lines over 16 KiB are skipped. A finding carries only the code and repo-relative `path:line` locations, never line text, and codes outside `[A-Za-z0-9_.:/+=-]{1,128}` are dropped with a stderr count. Because the scanner is `autoclose`, it fails the run rather than see less: an invalid `codes.json`, a `registry` or `docs` that matches no file, a file in both, an unreadable file, a failing tool or more than 5,000 findings all exit non-zero with no output. With no `codes.json` it logs `not configured` and succeeds with no findings, so deleting `codes.json` closes its open issues as \"No longer detected\". ard does not run it on itself until ard has error codes.\n216:**The docs scanner** (`scanners/docs`, a `sh` script plus one stdlib-only Go program, `docscan.go`, built at scan time with the job's own Go) lists undocumented exported Go identifiers. It reports, per package directory: a missing package comment; an exported function, a method on an exported receiver type (pointer, value or generic) and an exported type with no doc comment (the spec's doc, its trailing comment or the enclosing declaration's doc all count); and an exported const or var with no comment, one finding per parenthesised block that has no block comment. Struct fields, interface methods, methods on unexported types and comment wording are not checked. Every exported identifier counts, `internal/` included, and files for every platform count (build constraints are not evaluated). It skips `testdata/`, `vendor/`, `node_modules/` and dot or underscore directories, files whose names start with `_` or `.`, symlinks, `*_test.go`, `//go:build ignore` files, generated files and `package main`. The fingerprint is `docs:undoc:<dir>:<symbol>` or `docs:pkg:<dir>`; the file and line are left out, so moving a declaration within its package keeps its issue. Severity is `low` and the group is the package directory. It only parses files: it never runs repository code and needs no network. It fails closed: a parse error, unreadable file, invalid path or more than 10,000 findings fails the run with nothing filed. A human-owned `exclude` file in the role directory skips package directories (`dir/` is a directory and everything below it, anything else a glob matched against the package directory); an entry makes its findings vanish, so autoclose closes their issues. Go only for now.\n226:1. Query for the role's request label (the lead's is `ard:pending`), excluding claimed, done, failed, blocked and held issues. Which human-filed issues receive `ard:pending` is the `intake` setting (decided, #2): in `all` mode every human-filed issue enters with no label needed; in `label` mode only issues carrying the configured intake label do. `intake.Admit` decides, as a pure function of the issue, its timeline and the labeler's permission, and it fails closed. Issues authored by the App (scanner-filed) always pass. In `all` mode only issues opened after `all` mode was first enabled enter without a label; older issues still need the label. In `label` mode only the most recent `labeled` event for the label counts: it must come from the App or from a collaborator with role triage, write, maintain or admin (matched case-insensitively), and an earlier authorised event does not rescue a later unauthorised one. When the label is present but not honoured, the controller adds `ard:needs-human` with a short comment asking a maintainer to re-apply the label. The loop records each admission in the state `admissions` table (`labeled_by`, `labeled_at`, `body_hash`); if the title or body is edited after admission, the issue is re-gated with `ard:needs-human` and a comment until a maintainer re-applies the intake label. App-authored issues are exempt from the hash check. The loop never waits for a job: each claimed job is handed to a watcher goroutine that closes out through the shared Finisher, and a restart leaves running jobs to pool adoption and the Reaper. The re-gate hash covers the title and the body without ard's own cost block (see Guardrails, \"Intake is configurable\"), so ard's table writes never re-gate an issue. The first time ard writes the block, the stored hash stays valid; a maintainer relabel that falls between a human edit and ard's next table write may need repeating, because an ard body write bumps the issue's `updated_at`, which fails safe.\n238:**A deterministic gate starts the worker.** Once per triage tick, after the intake sweep, the controller searches for open issues that carry `ard:triage:lead:done` and none of `ard:pending`, `ard:needs-human`, `ard:hold`, `ard:executor:failed` or any configured worker's request label (the exclusion keeps gated issues from filling the 100-hit window). It re-reads each hit and decides from labels alone. \"Requested\" means the request label is present, \"done\" means `:done` is present. The gate waits while any triage job of the issue is running or any requested `ard:triage:<role>` lacks its `:done`; it leaves an issue alone once any `ard:worker:<role>` label exists (a worker already gated, started by a human, or in a fix round). When triage is finished it adds the lead's chosen `ard:worker:<role>`. The choice is read from the lead's close-out record in controller state, the value close-out validated, not from the committed `triage-lead.md`. It needs no pool slot and runs in the same conditions as the rest of the tick.\n240:Three cases go to a human, each with one comment and `ard:needs-human`, and nothing is ever closed automatically: the lead finished `done` but named no valid worker (or its close-out record is missing), the chosen worker role no longer exists, or an `ard:triage:<role>` is requested for a role that does not exist. A title or body edit after admission, on a human-filed issue, also stops the gate with a \"paused\" comment and `ard:needs-human`; re-admission by relabel is not offered, since a lead with `:done` is never re-claimed. A maintainer re-runs triage or adds the worker label.\n242:With `require_approval: true` (the Phase 1 default) the gate posts one \"ready for approval\" comment per lead job and waits. `ard:approved` is honoured only when its latest `labeled` event (reset by an `unlabeled`) is by a collaborator with triage permission or higher, is not the App, and comes after the last triage `:done` label of the issue. An approval given earlier is left in place and ignored: the gate waits for a fresh one and does not escalate. An approval from anyone else adds `ard:needs-human` with a comment that does not name the actor. A human who adds `ard:worker:<role>` directly bypasses the gate and has approved by doing so. The gate does not enforce that `triage:engineer` ran: the lead routes and the gate applies its routing. In `--dry-run` the gate logs `triage.would_gate` and writes nothing; otherwise each action records a `gate` event.\n278:**Agent CLIs plug in through adapters.** Each adapter turns (prompt file, context paths, budget) into a command line and parses the exit code and, where the CLI reports them, turns and cost. For Claude Code that is `claude -p --output-format=stream-json --verbose --restricted --safe-mode --strict-mcp-config --disable-slash-commands --permission-mode=dontAsk --settings='{\"includeGitInstructions\":false,\"disableAllHooks\":true}' --tools=… --allowed-tools=… --disallowed-tools=… --model <model> --max-budget-usd <max_budget_usd> --append-system-prompt-file <role prompt> -- <message>` (`--verbose` is required by `stream-json` in print mode). Every stage (scanner, triage, worker, review) gets the same tool profile: `--tools` and `--allowed-tools` are `Read,Glob,Grep,Edit,Write,Bash`, and `--disallowed-tools` is `Bash(git commit *),Bash(git push *),Bash(git remote *),Bash(gh *)` (decided, #79). ard does not pass `--bare`: it limits the CLI to Bash, Read and Edit, and `--tools` cannot widen it (#105). The planted-config isolation `--bare` gave comes instead from `--restricted` (only managed settings and `--settings` load), `--safe-mode` (no CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md, skills, agents, hooks, MCP servers or auto memory), `--strict-mcp-config`, `--disable-slash-commands`, and `--settings` that turns off hooks and the git status snapshot (commit subjects are third-party text). `CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_BACKGROUND_TASKS=1` is set as a fixed env var (the executor allows it) so Bash cannot detach commands. The integration tests pin each of these against a worktree with planted config and must be re-run on every `TestedVersion` bump. The tool list is not a security boundary, and the deny list is advisory, because shell indirection gets around it. The real boundaries are the missing GitHub token (Docker; see the host-mode note above, #84), the committer, which commits only `.ard/<issue#>/` from a triage or review job (other edits are discarded with the worktree), and the diff guard on worker changes. An explicit tool list from the caller (`Spec.Tools`) replaces the allow list and keeps the deny list. The user message holds only fixed text and the paths of the untrusted input files. The installed CLI accepts an undocumented `--max-turns`; ard does not rely on it, so a per-job dollar cap and the executor's hard timeout bound each run. Agents run with an empty per-job CLI config dir and HOME and receive exactly one model credential, passed by name by the executor and never visible to the adapter: an API key (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) or a subscription OAuth token (`CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN`, from `claude setup-token`); that rests on the environment, not on a CLI flag. `ard serve` chooses the credential from its own environment: `ARD_AGENT_AUTH` is `auto` (the default; the token if set, else the key), `oauth` or `apikey`, and an explicit mode never falls back to the other variable. Only one is ever passed, because the CLI prefers the API key and would silently bill it (pinned by an integration test). A missing credential does not stop startup, because deterministic scanners need none: `ard serve` logs one INFO line, and a job that needs one fails with a clear error. The executor creates the per-job config dir as `<job home>/.claude` (0700) in `prepare` (`Handle.View.ConfigDir`; under Docker it is a bind mount at `/ard-config`), refuses to start a job whose config dir is not that empty directory, and removes it with the job home; the Reaper's `KeepWorktree` retention keeps it too, and it holds no credential, since the credential travels only in the environment. `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBPROCESS_ENV_SCRUB=1`, which would keep the token out of Bash children, is not set (decided, #125). Residual risk: an API key (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) is inherited by Bash children; on the tested release the CLI does not pass `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` to them; and either credential can still be read from the `claude` process's own environment (`/proc/$PPID/environ` on Linux, `ps eww` on macOS). The container is the boundary: it holds no GitHub token, and the credential is scoped to the model. The scrub is off because on 2.1.328 it requires bubblewrap (and socat) plus unprivileged user namespaces; Docker blocks those under ard's flags (`--cap-drop ALL`, `no-new-privileges`, default seccomp), so every Bash call would fail closed and without bwrap the CLI refuses to start. On macOS host mode it worked and matched the `dontAsk` outcomes. It can be revisited for host mode or a loosened container. What agent Bash can read differs by mode: under Docker, only the job's own credential; in host mode, far more, because any same-uid process's environment is readable (`ps eww <pid>` on macOS, `/proc/<pid>/environ` on Linux), including `ard serve`'s own, which holds both credentials. The scrub would not close that; it is part of the host-mode risk of #84. `kimi` and `dsh-tui` both run headless with `-p`; neither has a turn cap and `kimi` reports no cost, so turns and cost are optional in the adapter result. `config.yaml` names the default CLI and any per-role override, so a role can move between CLIs without touching its prompt. The Claude adapter also reads a usage limit and token usage from the stream: a limit comes from a `rate_limit_event` with status `rejected` (its `resetsAt` is the reset time), from the CLI's own error text (`You've hit your session, weekly, Opus or Sonnet limit`) and from `rate_limit` or `billing_error` errors, and it records `usage` and `modelUsage` token counts (by input, output, cache write and cache read; per model when the result has `modelUsage`, which is preferred for accounting). That text is matched only on paths the CLI itself writes (an assistant message carrying an `error`, or an errored result with HTTP 429 or `api_error`), never in agent output, because an agent can be made to print the phrase. Only the job's own agent step and its fix steps are read for a limit: a worker's test step runs agent-written code, so its output is never classified (a printed limit line there cannot block the account). With an API key a 429 stays a transient `rate_limit` retry; only subscription (OAuth) runs have usage limits. The committed fixtures are scrubbed real captures where a human has made them (`testdata/CAPTURE.md`) and clearly named `synthetic_*` files elsewhere.\n282:**Close-out is resumable.** The controller (`internal/closeout`) decides the outcome from the role file (only one this job wrote), stages the redacted files, then commits, requests the `next` labels, posts one summary comment (for a review with a verdict, one PR review instead; either is tagged `<!-- ard:closeout job=<id> -->` so a replay never posts twice) and finally swaps `:running` for `:done`, `:changes`, `ard:needs-human` or `ard:executor:failed`. It records its phase in local state after each step. `serve` resumes unfinished close-outs at startup, and the Reaper resumes, rather than fails, a job whose close-out has started. Three more outcomes exist for a job that ended without a usable result: `failed` (sets `ard:executor:failed`), `retry` (a named transient cause: no `ard:executor:failed`, the request label kept, the next claim is the automatic retry) and `usage-limit` (a stop at a subscription usage limit: `:running` is removed, nothing is added, the request label stays, and the issue is claimed again after the reset without using up the retry). All three commit only the redacted log. Their comment carries the reason, the last 50 log lines (cut from the redacted log, scanned again and capped at 8 KiB; if that scan flags or fails, the comment says the tail was withheld) and when the worktree and host files are kept until. `next` entries are applied only for roles that exist; a `review:<role>` the lead requests is applied when the worker opens the PR, not at triage close-out. Only changed files under `.ard/<issue#>/` that the job's allowlist permits are committed, so a newer `issue.md` on `main` is never overwritten.\n303:1. **Poll and claim** `ard:worker:<role>` (added by the triage fan-in gate; the worker never adds or removes it) like triage, also skipping `ard:needs-human`, `ard:hold`, `ard:executor:failed` and an issue that has a running triage job or any running worker job. Workers rank second for job slots, after reviewers. Before claiming, the loop re-checks `ard:approved` when `require_approval` is on (no label change when it is missing), and the admission: an App-authored issue passes, a human-filed one passes only when its stored body hash still matches, and a changed title or body is re-gated exactly as triage does (one comment, `ard:needs-human`).\n304:2. **Branch** `worker/<role>-<issue#>-<slug>` from `origin/main`. The slug is the issue title lowercased, every run of other characters turned into one `-`, at most 40 ASCII characters, `issue` when nothing is left. The title can change between rounds, so the loop looks the branch up with `git ls-remote` on `worker/<role>-<issue#>-*`: one match is reused (and fetched), none is created on the first push, and more than one (only a human can make the second) posts once and adds `ard:needs-human` without claiming. Worker role names may not contain an all-digit segment (`eng-12`), so `worker/<role>-<issue#>-` names exactly one role and issue.\n307:5. **Guard the diff** after each agent or fix step, before anything leaves the host. The worktree belongs to the agent, so the guard never trusts its `.git`, config, index or attributes: git runs against the controller's own clone with a private index filled from the base commit (so `skip-worktree` and `assume-unchanged` hide nothing, and no planted filter, textconv, fsmonitor or hook runs), with its objects in a private temp directory (the clone is only read, so an agent cannot grow it); a worktree with a new or resized file over 1 MiB, or more than 256 of them, is held before anything is hashed. The scanned bytes are copied to a controller-private directory and the code lane commits the copies, so nothing can change a file between the scan and the commit. Close-out's changed-file listing uses the same hardened path. Paths under `.ard/<issue#>/` and `.ard-input/` belong to close-out and the loop and are left out. Everything else is checked, and any rejection holds the job for a human (`ard:needs-human`, nothing pushed): the path rule in `committer.CheckCodePath` (all of `.github/`, the rest of `.ard/`, `.git` segments, `.gitmodules`, any `.gitattributes` (`linguist-generated` or `-diff` would collapse files in the GitHub PR diff, so a worker change to one always goes to a human), secret-shaped names), any symlink, nested repository or other non-regular file, a file over 1 MiB, a binary file, and more than 256 changed files. The credential scan runs twice: the content of every written file is checked for the values of known secrets (the hold names the variable, never the value), and the built-in pattern scanner runs over it with only the findings on lines this job added kept, so a worker that edits a file that already holds a credential-shaped fixture is not held for lines it did not write. The comment names file, line and rule (`internal/foo_test.go:12: secret scan rule aws-access-key`), never the text. A scanner error fails closed. Test fixtures that look like credentials must be built at run time, as `internal/redact`'s tests do (`\"AK\" + \"IA\" + ...`); a literal is held, and there is no in-band escape. Over 1,000 changed lines in the whole PR (the diff against the merge base with `main`) also holds. The comment shows the reason as `diff guard: <entry>; <entry>; <entry>; and N more`.\n310:8. **Open or update a draft PR** with ard's own `Closes #<issue>`, the agent summary quoted and neutralised (`committer.NeutraliseRefs`, then marker and mention defusing), the test result and links to `worker-<role>.md`, the logs and the job id. Reviewers are chosen by `config.SelectReviewers`: the candidates are the role's `reviewers` (a list that only narrows), or every reviewer role when it is unset or empty, and each candidate's `when:` still decides. A `when:` matches when any path changed between the merge base of the config commit and the pushed head matches a `paths` entry, or a listed triage role ran (closed `done`, or its `ard:triage:<role>:done` label; `needs-human` does not count). Paths are case-sensitive and anchored at the repo root: `web/` is a directory, anything else a doublestar glob, so `**/go.mod` matches a root `go.mod` and `go.mod` only the root one; `.ard/` and `.ard-input/` paths are never selectable. Any `review:<role>` the lead deferred or the worker's own `next` lists is added regardless of `when:`, limited to roles that exist, and requested only when the tests did not fail. Selection is monotonic: each run recomputes from the whole PR diff and close-out only adds labels, so earlier reque","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"persistedOutputPath":"/ard-home/.claude/projects/-Users-al--ard-work-albertcbrown-ard-01M84R5GDB44NC8C0BT75DDE0V/b768ecb7-c87b-4dd5-9df7-ee39a31a7137/tool-results/bw9aocwy1.txt","persistedOutputSize":44791},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_017T7R9ZWprMGJtVNrDjJmfD","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
