{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DVGYUYayAuAwZJEGgJNsHv","type":"tool_result","content":"== 81\n| `ard:needs-human` | Controller, from the agent's result | Agent reported it is blocked; automation stops |\n== 175\n(required, one line, at most 200 characters), `severity` (required: `critical`, `high`, `medium`, `low` or `info`), `group` (optional; empty means one issue per finding), `files` (optional repo-relati\nve paths, clean, no `..`) and `body` (optional markdown, at most 64 KiB). An unknown key, a bad value, a duplicate fingerprint, a line over 1 MiB, a stream over 16 MiB or more than 10,000 findings is \nan invalid line, and **any invalid line fails the whole run**: nothing is filed, closed or changed, so a half-parsed run can never trigger autoclose. Titles, bodies and file names come from repository\n content, so the controller treats them as untrusted data: before they reach an issue body it escapes HTML comment delimiters, so scanner output cannot forge a marker.\n== 180\n2. **Backpressure, in code, before the scanner starts.** The controller counts open issues with `ard:scanner:<name>`; at or above `max_open` it skips the run and logs it, autoclose scanners included: \na scanner at its cap does not run with a zero filing budget, so it cannot autoclose its own fixed issues until a human or the stale closer frees a slot. A global cap across all scanners (`global_max_o\npen`) stops a bad day from burying the queue. A skipped run is not queued: it counts as a run, and the next scheduled run checks again.\n== 183\n5. **Group.** Findings that share a `group` key join one issue, up to `group_size` (N), as a checklist with one line per finding (ticked lines count towards N). An empty `group` means one issue per fi\nnding. New findings join an open group issue of the same group that has room and is not yet claimed, meaning it carries no `ard:` label besides `ard:scanner:<name>` and `ard:pending`; otherwise they s\ntart a new one. A regression never joins: it always opens a new issue, which may also hold new findings of the same group. The number of new issues per run is `min(max_open - open, global_max_open - g\nlobal open)`; joins are free. Findings over that budget are filed most severe first, and the rest stay unrecorded so the next run offers them again.\n== 184\n6. **File.** Edits and closes first, then joins, then new issues. Create the issue with `ard:scanner:<name>` and `ard:pending`, then queue `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` to the committer. The issue comes fi\nrst because its number names the directory. A committer failure is logged and does not undo the issue: its body already carries the checklist. **An issue with `ard:hold` is never edited, joined, comme\nnted on or closed**, and a close waits while any `ard:<stage>:<role>:running` label is on the issue, so a running job is not orphaned. Edits never rewrite `issue.md`, because after a claim the branch \nmay own `.ard/<issue#>/`.\n== 198\nroller loop that runs every 24 hours and is skipped while paused. It closes scanner issues still at `ard:pending` with no activity for `stale.inactive_for` (default 30 days), as not planned, with a co\nmment. Activity means a timeline event by anyone other than the ard App. It never closes an issue that has a `:running` label, `ard:hold`, `ard:needs-human`, a linked open PR, or a human author (any a\nuthor other than the App), and it closes nothing when the client is not authenticated as an App. It only comments and closes: suppression happens on the owning scanner's next reconcile, which suppress\nes every not-planned close for `stale.suppress_for`. A reopened issue gets a fresh comment when it goes stale again.\n== 422\nction` is for triage roles: the `## <heading>` of `plan.md` the role owns (one line, no leading `#`, unique across triage roles). The starter roles default to Requirements (product-manager), Design (a\nrchitect), UX (designer), Docs (documentation-planner) and Implementation (engineer), and a value in `config.yaml` overrides that; a triage role with none may write only its own role file. There are n\no per-role config files: `ard init` writes a role's starting overrides into `config.yaml` for the roles it ships. Once a second repo adopts ard, role directories can move to a shared roles repo pinned\n by ref, like a sidecar; a repo's own directory of the same name wins. `after` names triage roles, with no self-reference and no cycles.\n156:**All agent output is committed too.** Each run's full o\n443:- **Committed output is redacted.** Close-out strips kno\n156:**All agent output is committed too.** Each run's full output lands in `.ard/<issue#>/logs/<stage>-<role>-<ts>.log`. Close-out redacts known secret values from the whole stream and, in the same pass, replaces the controller host's own paths with fixed placeholders: the job worktree becomes `/work`, the job home `/ard-home`, the executor root `~/.ard`, the operator's home `~` and the controller's temp dir `$TMPDIR` (skipped when it is a shared `/tmp`). Each path is also replaced in its symlink-resolved form, longest first, and only where it ends at a path boundary; bare usernames are not touched. Close-out then secret-scans the whole redacted log (the scan sees the replaced text), then caps it at 1 MiB: the first 32 KiB plus the last ~992 KiB, cut on line boundaries and joined by one truncation marker line. The full redacted log stays host-local for 7 days. Git LFS is not used (decided, #4); revisit only if `.git` growth demands it. Once the redacted copy is committed, the raw log is deleted. Triage output goes to `main` through the committer; worker and reviewer output goes on the branch and ships in the PR. **Scanner run logs stay host-local in phase 0** (`~/.ard/logs/`, 7 days) and are not committed: one run can touch many issues, and close-out redaction and the secret scan (#20, #21) land after Gate 1. What a scanner run commits is `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` for each issue it files or joins, nothing else. Committing scanner logs, redacted, to each touched issue is a follow-up. Never committed: scanner state databases, and any log the secret scan flags. **Optional gitleaks pass (#89).** With `redact.gitleaks.enabled: true` (default false; `redact.gitleaks.path` names the pinned binary, default `gitleaks` on PATH, and is read at each close-out) the scan runs a second pass after the builtin one: `gitleaks detect --no-git --report-format json` over the redacted bytes, in a private temp dir with a scrubbed environment and a 2-minute timeout. Findings keep only the rule, line and column, never the matched text. The pass fails closed: a missing binary, an error exit, a timeout or an unreadable report means nothing is committed. A hit is treated like an internal hit: the log stays host-local for 7 days and the job gets `ard:needs-human`.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"72a1a7d6-7fdc-45ac-9b6e-7e6bb020d70e","uuid":"ce73693d-458c-4165-b6e1-85c7aa74558e","timestamp":"2026-11-23T17:48:52.219Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"== 81\n| `ard:needs-human` | Controller, from the agent's result | Agent reported it is blocked; automation stops |\n== 175\n(required, one line, at most 200 characters), `severity` (required: `critical`, `high`, `medium`, `low` or `info`), `group` (optional; empty means one issue per finding), `files` (optional repo-relati\nve paths, clean, no `..`) and `body` (optional markdown, at most 64 KiB). An unknown key, a bad value, a duplicate fingerprint, a line over 1 MiB, a stream over 16 MiB or more than 10,000 findings is \nan invalid line, and **any invalid line fails the whole run**: nothing is filed, closed or changed, so a half-parsed run can never trigger autoclose. Titles, bodies and file names come from repository\n content, so the controller treats them as untrusted data: before they reach an issue body it escapes HTML comment delimiters, so scanner output cannot forge a marker.\n== 180\n2. **Backpr